                <pb facs="0407" n="399" sameAs="frus:frus1941v04/pg399"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.94/2187: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Grew</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-08-27T17:00:00-04:00">August 27, 1941—5
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>542. Your 1290, August 22, 6 p.m. You will observe from the Department’s 527,
                    August 23, 11 a.m., which crossed your telegram under reference, that this
                    Government has endeavored to make clear to the public the absence of any
                    intention on the part of this Government to encircle or otherwise to threaten
                    any country.</p>
                <p>You may in your discretion indicate to officials of the Japanese Government that
                    the character of recent American press comment in regard to the situation in the
                    Far East represents a natural reaction to Japanese policies and courses of
                    action adversely affecting American interests. The Department feels that
                    positive action on the part of <pb facs="0408" n="400" xml:id="pg400"/> the
                    Japanese Government responsive to this Government’s representations looking to
                    respect by the Japanese Government for American rights and interests in the
                    Japanese Empire and areas under Japanese control might be expected to contribute
                    more to altering the trend of American public opinion to which the Director of
                    the American Bureau takes exception than any statements which the American
                    Government could make.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
